window.SKIM_PAPERS["1412.7449"] = {"concepts": [{"id": "parse-tree-linearization", "name": "Parse Tree Linearization", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "A constituency parse tree is turned into a flat token sequence by a depth-first traversal that emits an opening bracket-plus-label when entering a node and a matching labeled closing bracket when leaving it. This makes parsing a plain sequence-to-sequence task: input is the sentence, output is the linearized tree.", "explanation": "Constituency parsing normally outputs a tree. Sequence models output a string. Linearization is what bridges the two.\n\nThe traversal walks the tree depth-first, left to right. For each nonterminal node it writes an opening symbol $(X$ before that node's children and a closing symbol $)X$ after them, with terminals written in place.\n\nFor the sentence \"John has a dog .\" with tree S -> NP(NNP) VP(VBZ NP(DT NN)) . the output string is $(S\\;(NP\\;NNP\\;)NP\\;(VP\\;VBZ\\;(NP\\;DT\\;NN\\;)NP\\;)VP\\;.\\;)S$.\n\nThe mapping is invertible. Closing brackets carry their label and the traversal order is fixed, so a well-formed output string reconstructs exactly one tree.\n\nTwo preprocessing choices go with it. Part-of-speech tags are normalized to the single symbol XX, since POS tags are not scored by the standard constituency F1 metric; this reported about +1 F1. Trees are not binarized, and unary chains get no special treatment.\n\nOne effect of the encoding is a lopsided pair of vocabularies. The output vocabulary stays tiny — 128 symbols in the reported setup — against a 90K input word vocabulary with a single UNK for out-of-vocabulary words.\n\nNothing constrains the decoder to emit balanced brackets, so a small fraction of outputs are malformed: 1.5% of development sentences for a model trained on 40K sentences, and 0.8% for the large-corpus model. The fix used is to append or prepend brackets until the string balances. Failures concentrated on sentences that did not end in proper punctuation.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 2.2 (Linearizing Parsing Trees) and Figure 2; POS normalization and vocabulary sizes in Section 2.3; malformed-tree handling under \"Generating well-formed trees\" in Section 3.2.", "ownerPaper": "1412.7449", "sources": {"paperId": "1412.7449", "note": "Section 2.2 (Linearizing Parsing Trees) and Figure 2; POS normalization and vocabulary sizes in Section 2.3; malformed-tree handling under \"Generating well-formed trees\" in Section 3.2.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "wsj-parsing-protocol", "name": "WSJ Penn Treebank Evaluation Protocol", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "The standard constituency-parsing benchmark: train on the Wall Street Journal portion of the Penn Treebank (about 40K sentences), tune on section 22, test on section 23, and report bracketing F1 computed by the EVALB tool.", "explanation": "The Penn Treebank's Wall Street Journal section is the long-standing benchmark for English constituency parsing.\n\nThe conventional split is fixed, and every reported number assumes it. Training uses roughly 40K annotated sentences, about 1M tokens. Section 22 serves as the development set, about 1700 sentences. Section 23 is the test set.\n\nScoring is done by the EVALB program rather than by any per-token accuracy. EVALB compares the labeled brackets in the predicted tree against the labeled brackets in the gold tree, and reports precision, recall, and their harmonic mean F1. Part-of-speech tags are not part of that score. That is what makes it safe to discard POS labels during training — getting them right earns no credit.\n\nNow the size of the set. By neural-network standards 40K sentences is a very small supervised set — smaller than MNIST. So a result reported on \"WSJ only\" is as much a measure of data efficiency as of parsing skill, and results that use extra machine-parsed data are reported separately.\n\nAdditional gold treebanks can be pooled with WSJ to bring the human-annotated total to about 90K sentences. Those are OntoNotes v5, which itself contains the WSJ material, the English Web Treebank, and the corrected Question Treebank.\n\nTwo held-out sets are used alongside WSJ to test generalization. One is 1000 sentences from the Question Treebank (QTB). The other is the first half of each English Web Treebank domain (WEB), 8310 sentences. Each asks whether a parser trained on news transfers — to questions in the first case, to web text in the second.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Sections 3.1 (Training Data) and 3.2 (Evaluation), including the QTB/WEB held-out sets and footnotes on LDC corpus identifiers.", "ownerPaper": "1412.7449", "sources": {"paperId": "1412.7449", "note": "Sections 3.1 (Training Data) and 3.2 (Evaluation), including the QTB/WEB held-out sets and footnotes on LDC corpus identifiers.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "semi-supervised-parsing-corpora", "name": "Machine-Parsed Semi-Supervised Corpora", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "Two large synthetic training sets built by running existing parsers over unlabeled web news: a ~7M-sentence corpus labeled by one parser, and a ~11M-sentence \"high-confidence\" corpus kept only where two different parsers produced identical trees. Both are pooled with the ~90K gold sentences.", "explanation": "The recipe replaces human annotation with parser output. Unlabeled sentences are sampled from news appearing on the web. They are passed through existing constituency parsers, and whatever tree a parser produces becomes the training target for that sentence.\n\nThere are two such corpora, and they differ in how much filtering stands between the parser's output and the training set.\n\nThe first is the simpler one. A single parser — a reimplementation of the BerkeleyParser — labels about 7M sentences. Combined with the ~90K gold sentences, this set is called the BerkeleyParser corpus.\n\nThe second applies tri-training-style filtering. Two independently built parsers, a BerkeleyParser reimplementation and a ZPar reimplementation, parse the same sentences. Only the sentences where both produce exactly the same tree are kept.\n\nAgreement between two independent parsers is a cheap proxy for correctness. It is also length-biased: parsers agree far more often on short sentences than on long ones, so keeping only the agreements would skew the corpus short. To counter that, the kept sentences are re-sampled to match the sentence-length distribution of the WSJ training corpus.\n\nWhat survives is about 11M sentences, 250M tokens, again pooled with the ~90K gold sentences. This is the high-confidence corpus. Between the two corpora, that is roughly 17-18M machine-parsed sentences.\n\nThe claim these corpora support is that a student model trained on imperfect teacher labels can beat the teacher. The reported model reaches higher F1 than the parsers that generated its training data. The reason offered is that the student treats the teachers' errors as noise.\n\nOne caveat attaches to any comparison. Because these sets contain hundreds of millions of tokens, results on them are not directly comparable to prior supervised numbers; published semi-supervised and self-training results are the intended comparison points.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 3.1 (Training Data), paragraphs on the high-confidence corpus and the BerkeleyParser corpus; discussion of teacher/student in Section 6 (Conclusions).", "ownerPaper": "1412.7449", "sources": {"paperId": "1412.7449", "note": "Section 3.1 (Training Data), paragraphs on the high-confidence corpus and the BerkeleyParser corpus; discussion of teacher/student in Section 6 (Conclusions).", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "parsing-baseline-scores", "name": "Reference F1 Scores on WSJ", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "The F1 numbers this line of work is compared against, on WSJ section 22 (dev) and section 23 (test), split into WSJ-only supervised parsers and semi-supervised ones trained with extra unlabeled or machine-parsed data.", "explanation": "Comparisons in constituency parsing are made against a fixed table of published section-23 F1 scores. The table splits in two, and which side a result belongs on depends on what data it was allowed to train on.\n\nFirst the WSJ-only supervised side. The latent-variable BerkeleyParser scores 90.4. The linear-time shift-reduce parser of Zhu et al. (2013) also scores 90.4. A product-of-grammars ensemble reaches 91.8.\n\nThe semi-supervised column covers parsers that use self-training or extra unlabeled data. Zhu et al. score 91.3 there, as do Huang and Harper (2009). McClosky et al. (2006) reach 92.1, and a Huang and Harper (2010) ensemble 92.4.\n\nThe sequence-to-sequence results are reported alongside these. Trained on WSJ only, a single attention model reaches 88.3 and a 5-model ensemble 90.5. A non-attention baseline trained on the single-parser synthetic corpus also reaches 90.5. Trained on the high-confidence corpus, a single attention model reaches 92.5, and its 5-model ensemble 92.8.\n\nOne negative result does most of the argumentative work. A plain sequence-to-sequence model without attention, trained on WSJ only, does not reach a usable score — below 70 — even with dropout and early stopping. That is the main evidence offered for attention's data efficiency.\n\nDecoding uses a beam, and the width of that beam turns out to matter little. Beam size 10 is the reported setting. Cutting to beam 2 costs 0.2 F1, and greedy decoding costs 0.5 F1.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Table 1 and its discussion in Section 3.2, including the beam-size ablation.", "ownerPaper": "1412.7449", "sources": {"paperId": "1412.7449", "note": "Table 1 and its discussion in Section 3.2, including the beam-size ablation.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}], "items": [], "edges": [], "themes": [], "pages": [], "narrative": null, "insights": null, "refs": {"accessed": []}};
